WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.410
- Hey, everyone! What's up?

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.930
This is Luka Negoita.

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:05.240
I'm a data scientist and ecologist,

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.790
and I'm super stoked to
be launching my new course

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:10.711
on data visualization in R for ecologists.

00:00:10.711 --> 00:00:13.878
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:19.090
So, this is the course that I wish I had

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:21.150
as a grad student in ecology.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.230
If you're an ecologist or a biologist

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:25.360
and want to quickly
learn the essential tools

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:28.060
for making good figures,
this course is for you.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.770
I tried to summarize everything
together in this nice,

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:32.910
concise three-hour short course

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.450
that goes over all the basics

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:36.760
of plotting data
efficiently and effectively,

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.900
trying to reduce the R learning
curve as much as possible,

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.080
just to the essential tools that you need

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:43.950
to really create professional

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.530
and publication-ready figures in R.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:49.460
Through project-based
lessons and exercises,

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:51.010
I really just take you by the hand

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:53.450
beginning with the most basics

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:56.500
such as how to plot figures
using R's base package,

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.400
but then moving on to more
advanced topics such as ggplot2,

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.460
what it actually is, how it works,

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.510
and beginning to create
plots using ggplot2.

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:08.830
Specifically focusing on
five of the most essential

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:11.490
and common figures that
you find in ecology,

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.410
and how to plot them,
how to customize them,

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:14.540
how to annotate them.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:15.510
Along the way, of course,

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.410
we're also gonna cover how to
save and export your figures,

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:20.790
and then also some of the best practices

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:23.210
for creating publication-quality figures.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:26.490
Though this is an introductory
course to data visualization,

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.750
I do assume that you're
gonna be coming into it

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:30.630
with just a little bit of a background

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:32.170
in R and R Studio.

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:33.250
At least the basics,

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.920
knowing how to do some very
basic things with your data

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:37.910
will be important so that I don't...

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:39.860
because I'm not gonna
really spend a lot of time

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:41.700
on those topics in this course so...

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.450
and it's just gonna go right
into data visualization.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:45.930
If you don't really have that background,

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.530
if you're still starting from scratch

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:49.240
and don't really know what to do,

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.340
then definitely check out my other course

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:53.520
on the basics of R for ecologists,

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.650
which offers a complete foundation

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.490
and all the basics that you need to know

00:01:57.490 --> 00:01:59.230
for working with data in R.

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:01.340
And so, if you want to learn
more about that course,

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:02.890
there's a link down below

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:05.050
or actually maybe it's up above.

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:07.640
I'm not really sure where on
this website the links will be,

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:09.010
but somewhere on this website

00:02:09.010 --> 00:02:09.970
where you're watching this video,

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:11.510
there will be links for signing up

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:14.480
for the basics of R for ecologists,

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:15.670
as well as this new course

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:19.260
on the intro to data visualization
with R for ecologists.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:20.510
Thank you so much for watching.

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:21.740
Hope you enjoyed.

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:23.560
Please share this with all your friends,

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.600
with your university,
with your department,

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:28.140
whoever you think might
enjoy these courses

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.570
or benefit from them.

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:30.780
So, until then.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:31.613
Thanks again.

00:02:31.613 --> 00:02:33.093
And I'll see you there.

00:02:33.093 --> 00:02:37.093
(bright upbeat music continues)

